## Claim
Text: "You’re watching the cheaters and all those people that send in the phony ballots. … They want to have the count weeks after November 3."
Claim author: Donald Trump
Claim date: October 30, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did any credible investigation or court ruling find evidence of widespread fraudulent ballots being submitted in the 2020 U.S. presidential election?
Answer: No, the search result does not provide evidence of any credible investigation or court ruling finding widespread fraudulent ballots in the 2020 U.S. presidential election; instead, it states that "numerous national and state-level studies show that voter fraud is incredibly rare" and that the FBI director has said there is no evidence of widespread fraud.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/54562611

### Were there any documented instances of individuals being charged or convicted for sending phony ballots in the 2020 election?
Answer: Yes, the search result documents that in June 2020, New Jersey Attorney General Gurbir Grewal announced voting fraud charges against four individuals—including Paterson City Councilman Michael Jackson and Councilman-Elect Alex Mendez—for "criminal conduct involving mail-in ballots" during the May 12, 2020 special election in Paterson. However, these are charges, not convictions.

Source URL: https://www.nj.gov/oag/newsreleases20/pr20200625a.html

### Did any state or federal law require that all ballots be counted by November 3, 2020, or did states legally allow counting to continue after that date?
Answer: No federal law required that all ballots be counted by November 3, 2020, and states legally allowed counting to continue after that date. As the source notes, "election results are often not made official until weeks after Election Day," and states like Minnesota gave officials "an extra two days after Election Day to process absentee ballots," while provisional ballots "often remain uncounted for weeks after the election."

Source URL: https://bipartisanpolicy.org/report/counting-the-vote-during-the-2020-election/

### What was the actual timeline for counting ballots in key battleground states during the 2020 election, and did any state take weeks to complete the count?
Answer: Based on the source, no state took "weeks" to complete the final, certified count, but key battleground states like Michigan, Wisconsin, and Pennsylvania did not allow election officials to begin processing mail ballots before Election Day, which the report states "could take a long time" and predicted that "completely counting all ballots after the close of polls could take days." The source notes that while official certification could take weeks (e.g., "In some states like California, election results are not certified for nearly a month"), the initial race-calling delay in swing states was expected to take "days or longer," not weeks, for unofficial results.

Source URL: https://bipartisanpolicy.org/report/counting-the-vote-during-the-2020-election/

### Were there any systematic differences in how mail-in ballots versus in-person ballots were processed and counted in the 2020 election?
Answer: Yes, there were systematic differences in how mail-in ballots versus in-person ballots were processed and counted in the 2020 election. According to the Bipartisan Policy Center, "Ballots cast in person during early voting or on Election Day are validated for eligibility when the voter checks in to vote," whereas "the validation of mail-in ballots happens after they are returned by the voter," involving signature matching and other checks. Additionally, "it can take local election officials more time to count a larger than normal number of mail-in ballots," meaning that "reported mail-in ballot totals are not complete by the evening on Election Day."

Source URL: https://bipartisanpolicy.org/blog/by-mail-or-absentee-voting-is-safe-and-easy-and-those-ballots-count/

### Did the 2020 election see a higher volume of mail-in ballots compared to previous elections, and if so, did that affect the counting timeline?
Answer: Yes, the 2020 election saw a higher volume of mail-in ballots compared to previous elections due to COVID-19 concerns, which directly affected the counting timeline. As the source notes, "a higher percentage of ballots cast by mail means that it will take more time for election officials to carefully process and count all votes than in previous presidential elections," adding that "it can take local election officials more time to count a larger than normal number of mail-in ballots" and that "reported mail-in ballot totals are not complete by the evening on Election Day."

Source URL: https://bipartisanpolicy.org/blog/by-mail-or-absentee-voting-is-safe-and-easy-and-those-ballots-count/

