### Were there any legal challenges or public statements from election authorities that contradicted the claim that counting after November 3 was part of a cheating scheme?
Answer: Yes, the search result contains direct contradictions from election law experts and references to federal and state law. The article quotes constitutional law professor Edward B. Foley stating, “There are no official results on Election Night — there never have been,” and notes that “post-election day time to count absentee ballots, overseas military ballots, and provisional ballots are enshrined in both federal and state law,” including the federal deadline of December 8 for finalizing counts. This directly contradicts Trump’s claim that counting after November 3 is “totally inappropriate” and “not by our laws.”

Source URL: https://www.statesman.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/10/29/fact-check-is-counting-ballots-weeks-after-election-day-illegal/114534454/

## Final Judgement
The evidence from the fact-check shows that no credible investigation or court ruling found evidence of widespread fraudulent ballots in the 2020 election, and voter fraud is documented as extremely rare. The few charges filed (in Paterson, New Jersey) were isolated, not widespread. Counting ballots after November 3 is standard practice under federal and state law, with no requirement that all ballots be counted by Election Day. The increased volume of mail-in ballots due to COVID-19 led to longer counting times, and election officials and legal experts confirm that this process is legal and not indicative of cheating. No specific evidence was provided by the claimant to support allegations of phony ballots or a cheating scheme.

Based on this information, the claim that there were "cheaters" sending "phony ballots" and that counting after November 3 was part of a cheating scheme is clearly contradicted by the evidence.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check found no credible evidence of widespread fraudulent ballots in the 2020 election, with multiple studies showing voter fraud is incredibly rare and the FBI director stating no evidence of widespread fraud [BBC](https://www.bbc.com/news/54562611). The only documented charges (in Paterson, New Jersey) were isolated and did not involve convictions [NJ AG](https://www.nj.gov/oag/newsreleases20/pr20200625a.html). Counting ballots after Election Day is standard practice under federal and state law; there is no requirement that all ballots be counted by November 3, and states legally allowed counting to continue for days or weeks [Bipartisan Policy Center](https://bipartisanpolicy.org/report/counting-the-vote-during-the-2020-election/). The higher volume of mail-in ballots due to COVID-19 extended counting times, and election law experts confirmed that post-election counting is legal and not part of any cheating scheme [Statesman](https://www.statesman.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/10/29/fact-check-is-counting-ballots-weeks-after-election-day-illegal/114534454/). No specific evidence was provided by the claimant to support allegations of phony ballots or a cheating scheme.